      passage: "<p>When Priya's uncle called to say one of the barn cats had a litter nobody could place, she expected fluffy, easy kittens ready for a cardboard box on the porch. What arrived instead was a scrawny brown puppy, clearly not a kitten at all, that someone had left tied to the farm's mailbox with a note reading only \"please.\" The smallest of a litter dumped nearby, he had a crooked back leg and ribs Priya could count with her eyes. Her uncle didn't have time for a dog that needed bottle feeding and vet visits, so he asked Priya's mother if Priya, at twelve, was ready for the job. Priya said yes before her mother finished the sentence.</p><p>The first two weeks were harder than she had imagined. The puppy, whom she named Biscuit, woke her at three in the morning whimpering for a bottle, and he refused to walk more than a few steps on his weak leg before collapsing into the grass. Priya's older cousin Deshawn, who trained service dogs at the county shelter, warned her that rushing an injured animal only taught it to fear people. \"Small steps, every single day, at his pace, not yours,\" he told her, showing her how to massage the leg muscle gently and how to let Biscuit choose when to stand rather than lifting him upright herself. Priya wrote the schedule on an index card and taped it above her desk: feed, stretch, rest, feed, stretch, rest.</p><p>By the fourth week, Priya had grown impatient with how slowly things were going. She tried to speed things up one afternoon, tugging gently on Biscuit's leash to encourage him toward the porch stairs he had been avoiding. He yelped, pulled backward, and refused to come near her for the rest of the day, hiding under the kitchen table with his ears flat. Priya sat on the cold floor a few feet away, not reaching for him, just talking quietly about nothing in particular, the way Deshawn had once talked to a nervous shelter dog while Priya watched. It took almost an hour, but Biscuit finally crept out and rested his chin on her knee.</p><p>That evening Priya crossed out the word \"tug\" on her index card and wrote \"wait\" instead. Over the following months, Biscuit's leg grew stronger, though it never lost its slight crook, and he learned to climb the porch stairs on his own timeline rather than hers. Priya realized that caring for him had changed the way she waited for things generally, whether it was a hard math problem or a friend who needed space before talking. The night Biscuit finally raced up all four porch steps without pausing, Priya didn't rush to pet him. She let him come to her instead, tail spinning like a pinwheel, proud of himself in a way that made rushing seem like it had never been the point at all.</p>",
