      name: "Reading: Informational Text 1",
      passageTitle: "Catching Sunlight: How Solar Panels Power Our World",
      passage: "<p>Every second, the sun sends an enormous amount of energy toward Earth, far more than the entire planet uses in a year. Solar panels are one of the main tools humans have developed to capture a small slice of that energy and turn it into electricity we can use in homes, schools, and businesses. At the heart of every solar panel are dozens or even hundreds of small units called photovoltaic cells, usually made from a purified form of silicon, the same element found in ordinary sand. When sunlight strikes these cells, it knocks loose electrons inside the silicon, and that movement of electrons creates an electric current. This process, called the photovoltaic effect, was first observed in the 1800s, but it took until the 1950s for engineers to build cells efficient enough to be practical.</p><p>The electricity that comes directly out of a solar panel is direct current, or DC, but most homes and businesses run on alternating current, or AC. To bridge that gap, solar panel systems include a device called an inverter, which converts the DC electricity into the AC form that can power household appliances or feed into the local electrical grid. Many homeowners with solar panels also use a system called net metering, in which any extra electricity their panels generate during sunny hours gets sent back to the grid in exchange for credit on their electric bill, effectively letting the grid act as a giant battery.</p><p>Solar energy has grown dramatically over the past two decades, largely because the cost of manufacturing panels has dropped by more than eighty percent since 2010. Improvements in manufacturing, larger factories, and increased global competition among panel producers have all contributed to this price drop. Government incentives, such as tax credits for homeowners and businesses that install solar systems, have also encouraged faster adoption in many countries. Some critics argue that solar energy cannot fully replace fossil fuels because panels only generate electricity when the sun is shining, creating a problem for nighttime or cloudy-day power needs. Supporters respond that pairing solar panels with large battery storage systems, along with a mix of other renewable sources like wind, can address this gap, and that battery technology has been improving rapidly alongside panel technology.</p><p>Beyond rooftop installations, entire fields of solar panels, known as solar farms, now generate electricity for thousands of homes at once in many parts of the world. Some solar farms use panels mounted on structures that slowly rotate to track the sun's position throughout the day, capturing more sunlight than fixed panels. As the technology continues to improve and costs continue to fall, many energy experts predict that solar power will make up a steadily growing share of the world's electricity supply in the coming decades, even as debate continues over how quickly that shift should happen.</p>",
