      name: "Reading: Informational Text 2",
      passageTitle: "Stone by Stone: Building the Great Wall of China",
      passage: "<p>Stretching across northern China for thousands of miles, the Great Wall is often imagined as a single, continuous structure built all at once. In reality, it is a network of walls, watchtowers, and fortifications constructed and rebuilt over roughly two thousand years by many different dynasties, each facing different threats and using different building methods. The earliest sections date back to the seventh century BCE, when smaller rival states built walls to defend their own territories from rival kingdoms and northern nomadic groups. It was not until the Qin dynasty, around 221 BCE, that these separate walls were connected into a larger defensive line, though most of what visitors see today was built much later, during the Ming dynasty between the 1300s and 1600s.</p><p>Building methods varied enormously depending on the terrain and the materials available nearby. In the dry, rocky regions of the west, workers often used rammed earth, a technique in which soil is compacted layer by layer inside wooden frames until it hardens into a dense, durable wall. In mountainous eastern regions, where stone was more plentiful, workers quarried large blocks and fitted them together, sometimes reinforcing the structure with fired bricks, a technology that became widespread during the Ming dynasty because bricks were more resistant to weather damage than packed earth. Engineers also had to design the wall to follow the natural contours of mountain ridges, which made it far harder for enemy forces to approach but also made construction slower and more dangerous for the workers involved.</p><p>The Great Wall was never simply a barrier meant to be impossible to cross. Watchtowers placed at regular intervals along the wall allowed soldiers to signal danger to neighboring stations quickly, using smoke during the day and fire at night, creating a communication system that could relay a warning across great distances far faster than a messenger on horseback. Garrisons stationed along the wall also controlled trade routes, including sections of the Silk Road, allowing officials to tax merchants and inspect goods passing between regions. In this sense, the wall functioned as much as an economic and communication tool as it did a military barrier.</p><p>Constructing and maintaining such an enormous system came at a tremendous human cost. Historical records suggest that hundreds of thousands of laborers, including soldiers, peasants, and prisoners, worked on various sections over the centuries, often under harsh conditions with limited food and shelter, and many died during construction. Despite this difficult history, the wall's engineering achievements remain remarkable: builders adapted their techniques to dramatically different landscapes, created lasting structures using only the materials and technology available at the time, and produced a defensive and communication network that influenced the region's history for centuries. Today, portions of the wall have crumbled or been damaged by erosion and, in some cases, nearby construction, while other sections have been restored and preserved as historical and cultural landmarks.</p>",
